{0 ModelKit XGBoost}

The optional [modelkit-xgboost] package provides weighted CPU histogram-tree
regression and binary/multiclass classification, native missing-value routing, checked input
conversion, immutable specifications, and scoped native interoperability.
Installing [modelkit] does not install this package or its native dependencies.
ModelKit artifact codecs are not yet available.

{1 Installation and compatibility}

Install [modelkit-xgboost] separately. The package pins OCaml binding 0.2.1,
requires OCaml 5.2+, and admits native libxgboost major version three. The binding
uses a system libxgboost 3.0+ discovered through pkg-config or its vendored
source-build fallback; CMake, a C++ toolchain, and OpenMP are native build/runtime
dependencies. Windows is excluded by the binding. Local verification uses
OCaml 5.3.0, binding 0.2.1, and native libxgboost 3.0.4 and 3.4.2 on macOS arm64.
Separate pinned Python references use XGBoost 3.0.4 and 3.4.1. These combinations
are verified; other native releases require their own compatibility evidence.

{1 Immutable specifications}

{!Modelkit_xgboost.Specification.create} validates histogram-tree parameters.
[clone] preserves the unfitted specification and [params] returns immutable
values; parameter search builders create a fresh specification for each
candidate. Booster creation sets CPU execution, histogram trees, an RNG-derived
seed, and the configured [nthread]. It does not set a training objective or
train a model.

{@ocaml[
let specification =
  Modelkit_xgboost.Specification.create ~rounds:20 ~max_depth:3 ()
  |> Result.get_ok
let () = assert ((Modelkit_xgboost.Specification.params specification).Modelkit_xgboost.Specification.threads = 1)
]}

{1 Numeric admission}

{!val:Modelkit_xgboost.Input.features} copies a nonempty ModelKit matrix to
row-major float32 storage and retains its ordered feature schema. Native NaN
means missing; infinity is rejected. Regression targets and weights remain
finite after conversion. Already encoded classification labels must lie in
the range from zero inclusive to [class_count] exclusive and be exactly
representable as float32; learning the mapping from original labels is the
estimator's responsibility.

Float32 conversion is lossy. Each input reports its allocated float32 payload,
full-size temporary staging payload, source shape, and count of changed finite
values. These reports exclude native DMatrix/booster allocations and OCaml
headers. Finite feature/target underflow to zero is permitted and reported;
positive weights rounding to zero fail because that would change which rows
participate. Infinities and finite values rounding to infinity fail with typed
row/column context. Converted buffers are opaque and immutable to callers.

{@ocaml[
let x = Modelkit.Matrix.of_arrays [| [| 0.1; Float.nan |]; [| 2.; 3. |] |]
        |> Result.get_ok
let schema = Modelkit.Feature_schema.of_matrix x |> Result.get_ok
let converted = Modelkit_xgboost.Input.features ~feature_schema:schema x
                |> Result.get_ok
let report = Modelkit_xgboost.Input.feature_report converted
let () = assert (Modelkit_xgboost.Input.payload_bytes report = 16L)
let () = assert (Modelkit_xgboost.Input.rounded_values report = 1)
]}

{1 Fit and predict regression}

{!Modelkit_xgboost.Regressor} implements the public {!module-type:Modelkit.REGRESSOR}
protocol. Every fit builds independent native state, runs exactly the configured
number of rounds with squared-error loss, and returns a separate fitted value.
Optional sample weights are routed to the native DMatrix; zero weights remain
zero. Feature NaNs route through native missing-value handling. Training shape,
schema, target/weight alignment, and float32 admission fail with typed errors.

{@ocaml[
let y = Modelkit.Vector.of_array [| 1.; 2. |] |> Modelkit.Target.regression
        |> Result.get_ok
let weights = Modelkit.Sample_weight.of_array ~expected_length:2 [| 1.; 2. |]
              |> Result.get_ok
let fitted =
  Modelkit_xgboost.Regressor.fit specification ~sample_weight:weights
    ~rng:(Modelkit.Rng.create (Modelkit.Seed.of_int 41))
    ~feature_schema:schema ~x ~y ()
  |> Result.get_ok
let predictions, conversion =
  Modelkit_xgboost.Regressor.predict_with_report fitted ~feature_schema:schema ~x
  |> Result.get_ok
let () = assert (Modelkit.Target.length predictions = 2)
let () = assert (Modelkit_xgboost.Input.payload_bytes conversion = 16L)
]}

Prediction validates the fitted schema's exact names/order and feature count,
returns finite float64 values in source row order, and supports empty batches
with the fitted column count. {!val:Modelkit_xgboost.Regressor.report} exposes native
version, effective seed, rounds, and input conversion reports. Prediction's
conversion report describes only its float32 feature buffer; native output
storage and the returned float64 vector are additional allocations.

Fit detaches the learned model from training caches using an internal
XGBoost-produced model buffer, then releases the training booster and DMatrix.
The fitted value retains no training observations. It owns a GC-managed private
inference booster; native allocations are additional to OCaml heap accounting.
Admission costs linear time and float32 payload in the number of input values.
Cache detachment adds one model-buffer write/read during fit, with temporary
space proportional to model size. Prediction converts its feature batch once
and calls native in-place prediction. Calls sharing a fitted value serialize
native output copying; separate fitted values can predict independently.

Repeated fits/predictions are deterministic for fixed data, parameters, RNG,
native version, and thread configuration. Native sampling changes between
3.0.4 and 3.4.2, so the same seed alone does not reproduce a sampled model across
these versions. Pin the native runtime when reproducing training results. The
committed parity fixtures cover dense, weighted, missing/all-missing, sampled,
regularized, large-scale, constant-feature/target, single-row, and zero-learning
rate cases at absolute and relative prediction tolerances of [2e-6].

{1 Pipeline integration}

{!Modelkit_xgboost.Regressor.estimator} packages the immutable specification
using public ModelKit modules and reports package/version provenance. It accepts
routed sample weights and works as the terminal estimator of ordinary and
supervised pipelines. Add preprocessing through the usual pipeline APIs.

{@ocaml[
let estimator = Modelkit_xgboost.Regressor.estimator ~name:"trees" specification
                |> Result.get_ok
let pipeline = Modelkit.Pipeline.set_estimator Modelkit.Pipeline.empty estimator
               |> Result.get_ok
let fitted_pipeline =
  Modelkit.Pipeline.fit pipeline ~sample_weight:weights
    ~rng:(Modelkit.Rng.create (Modelkit.Seed.of_int 41))
    ~feature_schema:schema ~x ~y ()
  |> Result.get_ok
let predicted = Modelkit.Pipeline.predict fitted_pipeline ~feature_schema:schema ~x
                |> Result.get_ok
let () = assert (Modelkit.Target.length predicted = 2)
]}

This packaging currently reports [Pipeline.Unsupported] for portable artifact
encoding. The internal cache-detachment buffer is not a public ModelKit
pipeline codec; encoding a pipeline containing this estimator returns a typed
artifact error.

{1 Binary and multiclass classification}

{!Modelkit_xgboost.Classifier} implements the public {!module-type:Modelkit.CLASSIFIER}
protocol using the same immutable specifications as regression. Two effective
classes select binary logistic loss; three or more select softmax cross-entropy.
Fitting maps sorted original integer labels to exact contiguous float32 indices.
The mapping preserves negative labels and labels outside float32's exact integer
range. {!Modelkit_xgboost.Classifier.classes} returns a fresh copy of that sorted
vocabulary. Predictions return original labels; probability columns follow
exactly the same order. Exact ties select the lowest label, including binary
probability 0.5.

At least two classes with positive sample weight are required. Empty,
single-class, and single-effective-class targets return typed validation errors.
Zero-weight rows are excluded before native training and do not introduce new
classes or affect histogram cuts. Original feature values still pass the normal
float32/NaN admission policy. Classifier fit reports retain original input shapes
and expose original/effective row counts. If rows are filtered, feature and
weight conversion reports include full-input float32 staging, and the target
report includes label-export and row-selection array payloads. Label-mapping
tables, fitted class storage, native allocations, and model-detachment buffers
are excluded from conversion accounting.

{@ocaml[
let labels = Modelkit.Target.classification [| -7; 16777219 |]
let classifier =
  Modelkit_xgboost.Classifier.fit specification ~sample_weight:weights
    ~rng:(Modelkit.Rng.create (Modelkit.Seed.of_int 41))
    ~feature_schema:schema ~x ~y:labels ()
  |> Result.get_ok
let classes = Modelkit_xgboost.Classifier.classes classifier
let probabilities =
  Modelkit_xgboost.Classifier.predict_proba classifier ~feature_schema:schema ~x
  |> Result.get_ok
let () = assert (classes = [| -7; 16777219 |])
let () = assert (Modelkit.Matrix.shape probabilities = (2, 2))
let classifier_estimator = Modelkit_xgboost.Classifier.estimator specification
                           |> Result.get_ok
let classification_pipeline =
  Modelkit.Pipeline.set_estimator Modelkit.Pipeline.empty classifier_estimator
  |> Result.get_ok
let classification_model =
  Modelkit.Pipeline.fit classification_pipeline ~sample_weight:weights
    ~rng:(Modelkit.Rng.create (Modelkit.Seed.of_int 41))
    ~feature_schema:schema ~x ~y:labels ()
  |> Result.get_ok
let aligned = Modelkit.Pipeline.predict_proba classification_model
              ~feature_schema:schema ~x |> Result.get_ok
let () = assert (Modelkit.Matrix.shape aligned = (2, 2))
]}

Binary probability columns are one-minus-positive-probability and positive
probability for the lower/higher labels. Multiclass native probabilities must be
finite, lie between zero and one, and sum to one within [1e-6]; accepted rows
are normalized in float64 to remove float32 rounding. Invalid native output
returns a typed numerical error. Empty prediction batches retain their class
column count. Label-only prediction avoids allocating a full float64 probability
matrix. Classifier prediction shares the regressor's schema, native lifetime,
concurrency, and version-dependent reproducibility contracts. Mapping takes
linear time in rows plus class sorting; filtering adds linear staging in input
size, and multiclass prediction scales with rows times classes.

The estimator helper publishes sample-weight, class, and probability dispatch
with package/version provenance. Raw decision margins are not exposed. Pipeline
artifact encoding remains explicitly unsupported until its reviewed codec is
available. Pinned classifier references cover binary and multiclass dense,
weighted, missing/all-missing, sampled, regularized, constant-feature,
imbalanced, and zero-weight-class cases at absolute/relative [2e-6] tolerance.

{1 Native interoperability and lifetime}

{!Modelkit_xgboost.Native.with_dmatrix} validates target/weight alignment before
allocating native state, creates a fresh DMatrix, and releases it when the
callback exits. {!Modelkit_xgboost.Native.with_booster} similarly scopes a new
configured booster. Nest the booster scope inside its training DMatrix scope.
Callbacks borrow upstream handles: do not free them, retain them, or use them
after the scope ends. Binding failures become {!Modelkit.Error.t}; unrelated
callback exceptions propagate after cleanup. Returning a typed callback error
also releases both resources.

Booster thread limits do not constrain DMatrix construction. Binding 0.2.1 has
no constructor thread-limit argument. For bounded native concurrency, configure
OpenMP before starting the process, for example [OMP_NUM_THREADS=1] and
[OMP_THREAD_LIMIT=1] when parallel folds each use one native thread. This package
does not mutate process-global settings. [modelkit-parallel]'s [inner_threads]
value is diagnostic and does not configure OpenMP or XGBoost.
